\title{Any Contractible Map Is an Equivalence}
\taxon{Theorem}
\import{macros}
\p{
    For every map #{f:A\to B}, there is a function
    ##{
        \mathsf{isContrMap}(f)\to\mathsf{isEquiv}(f).
    }
}
\proof{
    Suppose every fiber of #{f} is contractible. For each #{y:B}, write the
    center and contraction as
    ##{
        (g(y),G(y)): \mathsf{fib}_f(y),
        \qquad
        C_y:\Pi_{z:\mathsf{fib}_f(y)}\bigl((g(y),G(y))=z\bigr).
    }
    The first components define #{g:B\to A}; the second components have type
    ##{
        G(y):f(g(y))=y.
    }
    Hence #{G:f\circ g\sim\mathsf{id}_B}, so #{(g,G)} is a section of #{f}.

    It remains to construct a retraction. Fix #{x:A}. The fiber over #{f(x)}
    contains both its chosen center and the canonical point
    ##{
        (g(f(x)),G(f(x))),
        \qquad
        (x,\refl_{f(x)}):\mathsf{fib}_f(f(x)).
    }
    Applying the contraction to the canonical point gives
    ##{
        q_x
        \defeq
        C_{f(x)}(x,\refl_{f(x)}):
        (g(f(x)),G(f(x)))=(x,\refl_{f(x)}).
    }
    Apply the first projection to this identity:
    ##{
        H(x)
        \defeq
        \mathsf{ap}_{\mathsf{pr}_1}(q_x):g(f(x))=x.
    }
    Therefore #{H:g\circ f\sim\mathsf{id}_A}, so #{(g,H)} is a retraction of
    #{f}. Pairing the section and retraction yields
    ##{
        \bigl((g,G),(g,H)\bigr):
        \mathsf{sec}(f)\times\mathsf{retr}(f)
        \defeq\mathsf{isEquiv}(f).
    }
}
